INT. PRISON VISITING ROOM - DAY

Gray tables bolted to the floor. A guard at the far door, unmoving. A vending machine humming in the corner. Fluorescent light with no shadow in it.

THEODORA MBEKI, 40s, in a tailored charcoal suit, sits upright with a legal pad squared before her. She has not written anything on it.

WINSTON PRICE, 60s, in prison-issue tan, enters. He sits across from her. His wrists are scarred where cuffs have been. His hands, flat on the table, are very still.

THEODORA
Mr. Price.

WINSTON
Ms. Mbeki.

THEODORA
You look rested.

WINSTON
I look like a man with nothing to do.

THEODORA
The appeal has been filed.

WINSTON
So I'm told.

THEODORA
Do you have questions?

WINSTON
Only one.

THEODORA
Go ahead.

WINSTON
If they let me out, where do I go?

Theodora's pen stops above the pad.

THEODORA
Home, I'd hope.

WINSTON
There's nobody home.

THEODORA
There's the house.

WINSTON
Someone sold the house.

THEODORA
It may be recoverable.

WINSTON
It may.

A long pause. The vending machine hums. A coin drops somewhere.

THEODORA
Mr. Price, I'm going to be honest with you. The odds are one in three.

WINSTON
That's better than I expected.

THEODORA
It's worse than I'd like.

WINSTON
You always say that.

THEODORA
It's always true.

Winston looks at her hands. At her wedding ring. At the way her thumb worries the cap of the pen.

WINSTON
Your husband still in Cleveland?

THEODORA
How did you know about Cleveland?

WINSTON
You told me once.

THEODORA
I don't tell clients where my husband lives.

WINSTON
You told me. In the second year. You said it quietly, like you'd been meaning to for a while.

Theodora puts down the pen. Folds her hands.

THEODORA
He's in Cleveland.

WINSTON
Good.

THEODORA
Why is that good?

WINSTON
Because one of us should have somebody waiting.

She looks at him for a long moment. When she speaks, her voice is lower.

THEODORA
Mr. Price, you will have somebody.

WINSTON
Will I?

THEODORA
You will have me. Until the day they open that gate.

He nods, slowly. Almost smiles.

WINSTON
That's good enough.

The guard clears his throat. Theodora rises, gathers her legal pad, and, after a moment's hesitation, leaves a pen on the table.

THEODORA
In case you think of something to write.

She walks to the door. Does not look back.
